24664,406.399780,49.946350,406.389221,253.299881,406.389221,49.946350,395.457764,253.299881,395.457764,38.154404,395.447205,283.159485,395.447205,38.154404,384.515747,283.159485,384.515747,49.946350,384.448364,171.874039,384.448364,49.946350,373.516907,171.874039,373.516907,38.154404,373.506348,283.128357,373.506348,38.154404,362.574890,283.128357,362.574890,49.946350,362.564331,283.149048,362.564331,49.946350,351.632874,283.149048,351.632874,49.946350,351.622314,283.126038,351.622314,49.946350,340.690857,283.126038,340.690857,49.946350,340.623535,102.024551,340.623535,49.946350,329.692078,102.024551,329.692078" rect="37.755997,329.350464,283.518219,636.854004" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>In conclusion, LBSI are experienced by approximately 5.4&#xD;in every 100 elite Australian cricket players per season, with a&#xD;high time cost of approximately 4 to 8 months to return to&#xD;match-level bowling. Injuries are most common in male pace&#xD;bowlers which is the cohort who has received most attention&#xD;in research to-date; however, LBSI have been observed in&#xD;females and nonpace bowlers. Recurrent LBSI account for&#xD;one-third of all LBSI, with a higher time cost compared with&#xD;new LBSI. Nonunited defects predispose subsequent pain,&#xD;LBSI, and spondylolisthesis. The findings of this study&#xD;reinforce the importance of early detection and conservative&#xD;individualized management of LBSI, particularly recurrent&#xD;LBSI, which may be supported by MRI.&#xD;Clinical recommendations based on the findings of this&#xD;study and the authors’ experience are as follows:&#xD;1. MRI should be used proactively for the early detection of&#xD;LBSI, either as a screening tool in higher risk athletes, or as&#xD;soon as symptoms consistent with LBSI develop.&#xD;2. Upper lumbar vertebrae LBSI should be suspected as they&#xD;occur in approximately one in 3 cases.&#xD;3. Management of LBSI should aim for healing, allowing&#xD;longer time for more severe and/or recurrent LBSI.&#xD;4. MRI may be used to monitor bone healing and inform&#xD;progression to return to sport.&#xD;5. Athletes identified with a nonunited defect should be&#xD;treated with a high index of suspicion of complications&#xD;such as contralateral injury or ongoing symptoms at the site&#xD;of the defect.</span
></p
